          'Duke Ellington put the point in a song title in 1931: “It Don’t Mean a Thing (If It Ain’t Got That Swing)”. It sounds like a slogan, but it states a standard of judgement. A solo can be clever, fast and harmonically correct and still fail if it does not swing, because jazz musicians judge a performance first by how it feels in the body. Lester Young, Count Basie and Billie Holiday are often admired less for any single note than for where they put their notes in time.',
