          'Ralph Ellison defined the blues in his 1945 essay on Richard Wright as an impulse to keep the painful details of a brutal experience alive in memory, to finger their jagged grain, and to move past them not through philosophy but by squeezing from them a “near-tragic, near-comic lyricism”. That phrase describes something you can hear. A blues song names exactly what is wrong, often with dark humour, and in the act of naming it gains some power over it.',
          'This is why blues lyrics are so specific. “Pony Blues”, “High Water Everywhere”, “Parchman Farm Blues” are about particular places, events and people. Charley Patton’s “High Water Everywhere” (1929) describes the 1927 Mississippi flood that displaced hundreds of thousands of people, many of them Black sharecroppers forced to work on the levees. Bukka White’s “Parchman Farm Blues” (1940) came from his own time at the Mississippi State Penitentiary. The singer is a witness, not just a sufferer.',
          'The blues is also personal in a way earlier Black American song often was not. Amiri Baraka argued in Blues People (1963) that the blues as a solo, secular song grew after emancipation, when freed people gained, however narrowly, some private life, mobility and leisure. Spirituals and work songs were collective; the blues is one person telling their own story. That shift from “we” to “I” is part of what the music means.'
