          'Angela Davis, in Blues Legacies and Black Feminism (1998), argued that these singers created a space where working-class Black women could talk openly about sexuality, independence and abuse. Ma Rainey’s “Prove It on Me Blues” (1928) hints at love between women; other songs describe leaving a bad man or taking revenge. The blues treats the body and desire as facts of life, not shameful secrets, and this frankness is part of its ethic of honesty.'
