          'Henry Louis Gates Jr., in The Signifying Monkey (1988), described signifying as a Black American practice of indirect, playful and often critical speech. Blues lyrics signify constantly: a song about a “mean old train” may be about leaving, a song about a car about sex, a complaint about a boss can be hidden inside a love song. When you sing or play the blues, you are taking part in this tradition of saying more than one thing at once.'
