          'The blues was made by Black Southerners, mostly poor, in a society built on segregation, sharecropping and violence. Many of its musicians were cheated by record companies and publishers. In the 1950s and 1960s, white musicians in Britain and America built careers on blues songs, sometimes without credit; Willie Dixon later won a settlement over Led Zeppelin’s “Whole Lotta Love”. This history matters to how the music is taught and played today.',
