          'Jazz manouche harmony uses 6 and 6/9 chords instead of major 7ths, and m6 chords for minor. The m6 chord is very flexible: Am6 has the same notes as F♯m7♭5 and can substitute for D7 without its root. Diminished 7th chords are used as passing chords and as substitutes for dominant 7♭9 chords: E7♭9 can be played as an Fdim7, G♯dim7, Bdim7 or Ddim7, since they share notes.',
          'Many tunes are in minor, using i–iv–V7 progressions like “Minor Swing” (Am6–Dm6–E7). Major tunes often come from American standards. The harmony is simpler than bebop but rich in colour.',
          'Because a dim7 chord repeats every three frets, it is the guitarist’s best friend in this style. A single diminished shape slid up in minor thirds gives a dramatic run between two chords, and many intros and endings are built this way. The m6 shape does the same work in minor: played on the middle strings it has a dark, sweet sound that defines tunes like “Minor Swing” and “Dark Eyes”. Learn to hear these colours: the 6th instead of the major 7th, the m6 instead of the m7, sounds older and more songlike.'
