          'Soloing relies on arpeggios, especially of the chord being played, with chromatic approach notes from a half step below. Fast diminished runs, octaves, tremolo on a single note and glissando sweeps across strings are signatures of Django’s style. French players call licks plans, and learning Django’s plans is a key part of study.',
          'Django’s two-finger technique led him to play arpeggios across strings and move horizontally along the neck. Many players today use all four fingers but keep his approach to shapes and phrasing.',
          'Rhythm matters inside the lead too. Django often played triplet phrases against the swing, repeated a short figure across the bar line, or held a single note with tremolo picking to build tension before a run. His fast runs usually end exactly on a chord tone on a strong beat, so the speed feels like an arrival, not a blur. Octave melodies, which he used long before Wes Montgomery made them famous in jazz, give a fat, singing tone on the dry Selmer-type guitar.',
          'A useful habit is to think of each solo as a set of short songs. Play a phrase, answer it, leave a gap for the rhythm guitars, then play a longer phrase. Modern players such as Stochelo Rosenberg are very fast, but the best of them still phrase like singers.'
